course: geography
lesson: 17
title: Mountains and highlands
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most great mountains are pushed up where pieces of the Earth's crust crash together very slowly: India is still pushing into Asia, so the Himalayas are still rising by a few millimetres a year.
explain: «Highest» and «tallest» are not the same: Everest has the highest summit above sea level, but Mauna Kea in Hawaii is taller from its foot on the sea floor, and Chimborazo in Ecuador reaches farthest from the Earth's centre because the Earth bulges at the equator.
explain: The higher you climb, the colder it gets, about 6.5 degrees for every 1,000 metres; that is why snow lies on Kilimanjaro even near the equator.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5, mountains are mostly places where attacking armies suffer, and the Alps, the Andes of the Inca and the high, poor provinces of Tibet all appear. But real mountains also make weather: they catch the rain on one side and leave a dry «rain shadow» on the other.
explain: Think of a car climbing a mountain road on holiday: the trees get smaller, the air gets colder, your ears pop, and at the top there may be snow in summer.
fact: Mount Everest, on the border of Nepal and China, is the highest mountain above sea level at 8,849 metres, a height agreed by both countries in 2020.
fact: All 14 mountains higher than 8,000 metres are in the Himalaya and Karakoram ranges of Asia, and K2, at 8,611 metres, is the second highest.
fact: The Andes, running about 7,000 km down western South America, are the longest mountain range on land, and Aconcagua, at 6,961 metres, is the highest peak outside Asia.
fact: Mauna Kea in Hawaii rises over 10,000 metres from its base on the sea floor, taller than Everest, though only about 4,200 metres stick out above the sea.
fact: The summit of Chimborazo in Ecuador is the point on Earth farthest from the planet's centre, even though it is lower above sea level than Everest.
fact: The Alps arc across eight countries, and Mont Blanc, on the French-Italian border, is their highest peak at about 4,800 metres, its exact height changing slightly with its ice cap.
fact: Denali in Alaska, at 6,190 metres, is the highest in North America, Kilimanjaro in Tanzania, at 5,895 metres, is the highest in Africa, and Mount Vinson, at 4,892 metres, is the highest in Antarctica.
fact: Air gets colder by about 6.5 degrees Celsius for every 1,000 metres you climb, and on top of Everest each breath holds only about a third as much oxygen as at sea level.
fact: Mountains force moist air to rise and drop rain on the windward side (the side the wind blows onto), leaving a dry rain shadow on the other side.
fact: The Tibetan Plateau, averaging more than 4,500 metres high, is the highest large plateau on Earth, and because it catches snow and rain that run off in every direction, the Yangtze, Yellow, Indus, Brahmaputra and Mekong start on or near it.
fact: The Scottish Highlands and the Appalachian Mountains of North America are parts of the same ancient mountain chain, formed over 400 million years ago before the Atlantic Ocean opened.
fact: The Ural Mountains, about 2,500 km long, are old, worn-down mountains commonly used as part of the boundary between Europe and Asia, and their highest peak is under 1,900 metres.
exercise: How high is Mount Everest above sea level? | 8,849 metres | 4,800 metres | 10,000 metres | 6,961 metres
exercise: Which two countries agreed Everest's height in 2020? | Nepal and China | India and Pakistan | Nepal and India together | China and Bhutan
exercise: Where are all 14 mountains higher than 8,000 metres? | In the Himalaya and Karakoram ranges of Asia | Spread out over all seven continents, at least one on each | In the Andes of South America | In the Alps and the Urals
exercise: Which is the second highest mountain in the world? | K2 | Mont Blanc | Kilimanjaro | Aconcagua
exercise: Which is the longest mountain range on land? | The Andes | The Himalayas | The Alps | The Urals
exercise: Which is the highest peak outside Asia? | Aconcagua | Denali, in Alaska | Mont Blanc | Kilimanjaro
exercise: Why can Mauna Kea be called taller than Everest? | Measured from its base on the sea floor it rises over 10,000 metres | Its summit is higher above sea level | It grows a whole metre every year because it is an active volcano in the sea | It is measured with its snow cap and the clouds on its top
exercise: Why is Chimborazo's summit the farthest point from the Earth's centre? | The Earth bulges at the equator, and Chimborazo stands near it | It is the highest mountain above sea level, higher even than Everest | It is a volcano that keeps growing | It stands at the North Pole
exercise: What is the highest peak of the Alps? | Mont Blanc | The Matterhorn | The Zugspitze | Mount Olympus
exercise: Which is the highest mountain in Africa? | Kilimanjaro | Mount Kenya | Mount Atlas | Table Mountain
exercise: Which is the highest mountain in North America? | Denali | Mount Whitney | Mount Rainier | Mount Washington
exercise: How much colder does the air get for every 1,000 metres you climb? | About 6.5 degrees Celsius | About 65 degrees Celsius | About 1 degree Celsius | It gets warmer, not colder
exercise: How much oxygen does each breath hold on top of Everest compared with sea level? | About a third | About twice as much | Exactly the same | None at all
exercise: What is a rain shadow? | A dry area on the far side of mountains from the rainy wind | The cool shade under a big rain cloud on an otherwise sunny day | A valley that floods every spring when the snow melts | A cloud that never drops rain
exercise: Which is the highest large plateau on Earth? | The Tibetan Plateau | The Sahara Plateau | The Swiss Plateau | The Brazilian Highlands
exercise: How can mountains in Scotland be related to mountains in America? | They were one chain before the Atlantic Ocean opened | A great river once carried rocks all the way from America to Scotland | People moved rocks across on ships | They are not related in any way
exercise: Why are the Urals much lower than the Himalayas? | They are very old and have been worn down over time | They are young and have only just started to grow from nothing | They are made of soft snow that melts every summer | They lie under the sea
test: Ruby says: «Everest is the tallest mountain, end of story.» What is the best answer? | Everest is the highest above sea level, but Mauna Kea is taller from its base | Ruby is right in every way | Kilimanjaro is the tallest, because it stands alone on a flat plain | K2 is the tallest, and Everest comes second
test: «snow on kilimanjaro?? it's in africa, it's boiling» Which is the best answer? | Air gets much colder high up, so snow can lie on its summit near the equator | It is fake snow made by machines every night for the tourists who come to climb it | Kilimanjaro is near the South Pole | Africa has very cold summers
test: In Europa Universalis 5, mountains mostly hurt attacking armies. What else do real mountains do that the game hides? | They make weather, catching rain on one side and leaving a dry rain shadow | Nothing, they only stop armies | They make the ground hotter at the top, closer to the Sun | They stop rivers from forming anywhere near them, so valleys stay dry
test: What would happen to the temperature if you climbed 3,000 metres up from a beach at 25 degrees? | It would be roughly 20 degrees colder at the top | It would be about 20 degrees warmer, being closer to the Sun | It would stay at 25 degrees | It would be 3,000 degrees colder
test: «why is one side of the mountains green and the other side all dry» What is the reason? | Moist air rises and drops rain on the windward side, leaving a rain shadow behind | The Sun shines only on one side, so the plants on the other side can never grow | Animals eat all the plants on one side | The dry side is always higher
test: «the himalayas are done growing, they're ancient innit» What is right? | They are young mountains, still rising as India pushes into Asia | They stopped growing a million years ago and are wearing down | They are shrinking a metre a year | They are older than the Urals
test: «why do so many big rivers start in one place, tibet?» Which is the best answer? | The high plateau catches snow and rain, and water runs off in every direction | Tibet has a giant pump | Rivers come from the sea up to Tibet through tunnels under the mountains of India | All rivers in the world start in Tibet
test: Why does the height of Mont Blanc change slightly from year to year? | Its summit is covered by an ice cap that grows and shrinks | The mountain sinks every winter | People keep cutting off the top | It is a volcano that erupts every year and adds new rock to its top
test: «ok which is the highest in antarctica, mount vinson or what» What is right? | Mount Vinson, at 4,892 metres | Mount Everest | Mount Erebus, at 8,000 metres | Antarctica has no mountains
test: «my mate climbed everest without trouble breathing lol» What is most likely true? | The air on top holds only about a third of the oxygen, so breathing is very hard | Breathing is easier at the top | The air is the same as at sea level | There is more oxygen at the summit, because the air there is cleaner and fresher
test: «ural mountains, are they in europe or asia» Which is the best answer? | They are commonly used as part of the boundary between Europe and Asia | They are in Africa | They are entirely in western Europe, between France and Germany | They are in South America
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between the highest and the tallest mountain on Earth? | Everest possesses the greatest elevation above sea level, whereas Mauna Kea is the tallest measured from its base | Mount Everest possesses both the greatest elevation and the greatest height from base | Mauna Kea possesses the greatest elevation above sea level | Kilimanjaro possesses the greatest height measured from any base
exam: Identify the principal reason for the diminished temperature prevailing at the summits of high mountains. | The air cools by about six and a half degrees Celsius for every thousand metres of ascent | The summits are situated at a greater distance from the Sun, whose heat diminishes with altitude | Snow at the summit generates cold of itself | The summits receive no sunlight whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the mountain range within which all fourteen summits exceeding eight thousand metres are situated. | The Himalaya and Karakoram ranges of Asia | The Andes of South America | The Alps of Europe | The Rocky Mountains of North America
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the phenomenon of the rain shadow. | Moist air, forced upward by mountains, discharges its rain upon the windward side, leaving the leeward side arid | Rain falls exclusively upon the leeward side | Mountains generate rainfall of themselves irrespective of the wind | The rain shadow is the shade cast beneath a cloud
